<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  The simple control run: from the stick to the surfaces, for every simple airplane, for JSBSim v1.3.3.
  FSG's own file: new in FSG, milestone 3 re-planned (docs/milestones/3-replan.md, pull request 3). An
  airplane's own file loads it with
      <system file="../simple/controls"/>
  beside the simple physics (aircraft/simple/aero.xml, which lists the numbers the airplane declares and
  says where each rule came from).

  What it is. The owner's design (28 November 2026): "Tilt moves the control surface directly, through one
  curve against speed, as cables and pushrods would: slow, it moves a long way and does little; fast, it
  moves a little and does a lot. No modes." The curve is Wings' rule (135crewdog/wings, src/sim/aero.js,
  elevatorPower and aileronPower, commit 6d689b7), written here as how far the surface moves:
    Pitch   Full back stick is all of the stabilator's travel below corner speed, where the wing stalls
            before the g limit; faster, it is only as much travel as holds the g limit in a turn (the pull
            back to the trim angle at that g's angle of attack, and the damping at that turn's pitch rate).
            Forward stick has the stabilator's smaller travel the other way, and when fast as much of it as
            holds the negative g limit.
    Roll    Full stick is all of the ailerons' travel up to the roll rate's speed; faster, less, so that
            the roll rate holds instead of growing.
    Yaw     The rudder goes where it is asked. The simple airplanes have no pedals: in the air the copilot's
            feet ask (src/sim/assists.js), and on the ground the tilt does (below).
  The trim and the stick are summed before the curve. So they add one for one, which is what the tests'
  airborne start assumes when it turns JSBSim's own trim into a stick position (src/sim/game.js,
  Flight.start); and the trim, like the stick, moves the stabilator less the faster the airplane goes.

  In: JSBSim's own commands, fcs/elevator-cmd-norm (positive nose down), fcs/pitch-trim-cmd-norm,
  fcs/aileron-cmd-norm (positive right), fcs/roll-trim-cmd-norm, fcs/rudder-cmd-norm (positive nose left).
  Out: JSBSim's own names for the surfaces, which the game's state and the physics read:
  fcs/elevator-pos-rad, fcs/left-aileron-pos-rad, fcs/right-aileron-pos-rad, fcs/rudder-pos-rad (and each
  in degrees). The ailerons are written as an ordinary pair, one down and the other up by the same angle.

  On the ground, tilt steers (pull request 4). The owner's rule (28 November 2026): "On the ground, tilt
  steers: rudder and nosewheel, blended by speed. It does not move the ailerons."
    With any wheel on the ground the tilt is the pedals: the rudder, all of its travel at full tilt, and
    the nosewheel, less and less of its travel the faster the airplane rolls. The ailerons are neutral. The
    pedals there are the pilot's alone: the rudder the copilot asks for counts only in the air.
    In the air the tilt is the ailerons, the rudder is the copilot's, and the nosewheel is straight.
    The two swap over a third of a second as the wheels leave or touch, so nothing jumps: one number,
    simple/on-the-ground, 1 on the wheels and 0 flying, moved from one to the other at a steady rate by
    JSBSim's own <kinematic> (the part that times the landing gear's travel) on JSBSim's own gear/wow.
  How others steer with no pedals: MSFS and X-Plane 11 tie the rudder and the nosewheel to the roll stick,
  and the ailerons still move; Wings steers the nosewheel with the tilt, fading with speed. Leaving the
  ailerons out on the ground is the owner's choice, for what milestone 3 measured: one tilt doing three
  jobs, so that steering at 60 knots also rolled the airplane (docs/milestones/3-replan.md, section 1).
  The nosewheel's fade is the curve FSG already had (src/sim/assists.js, wheelShare, with the numbers of
  src/sim/aircraft/f4c.js: a half at 28 knots, power 3; after the flight manual's "use nose gear steering
  until the rudder becomes effective at approximately 70 knots"). It is written once more here because
  the swap is here: tests/simple-f4c-ground.test.js holds the two curves together.
  The nosewheel itself is JSBSim's own: it turns as fcs/steer-cmd-norm asks, to the limit the airplane's
  file gives it (max_steer), and the game reads its angle as fcs/steer-pos-deg.
-->
<system name="simple controls">

 <!--
  The speed curve in pitch, back stick: the share of the stabilator's travel that full back stick gets,
  1 when slow, less when fast. Wings' elevatorPower, with this file's names:
    the angle of attack the g limit takes at this speed, but no more than the full-back angle;
    the g that angle really holds, and the pitch rate of a level turn at that g;
    the moment that holds both: the nose's stiffness over the way from the trim angle, and the damping.
  Over the tail's power, that is the share. Slow, the stiffness part alone is the whole of the tail's power
  (that is how the stiffness is set, aero.xml), so the share is 1.
 -->
 <function name="simple/angle-per-g-rad">
  <quotient> <property> inertia/weight-lbs </property> <product> <property> simple/qs </property> <property> simple/lift-slope </property> </product> </quotient>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/back-angle-rad">
  <min> <product> <property> simple/g-limit </property> <property> simple/angle-per-g-rad </property> </product> <property> simple/full-back-angle-rad </property> </min>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/back-g">
  <quotient> <property> simple/back-angle-rad </property> <property> simple/angle-per-g-rad </property> </quotient>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/back-moment">
  <sum>
   <product>
    <value> -1 </value> <property> simple/nose-stiffness </property>
    <difference> <sin> <property> simple/back-angle-rad </property> </sin> <sin> <property> simple/trim-angle-rad </property> </sin> </difference>
   </product>
   <product>
    <value> -1 </value> <property> simple/pitch-damping </property> <property> simple/c2v </property>
    <quotient>
     <product> <value> 32.174 </value> <max> <value> 0 </value> <difference> <property> simple/back-g </property> <quotient> <value> 1 </value> <max> <property> simple/back-g </property> <value> 1 </value> </max> </quotient> </difference> </max> </product>
     <property> simple/v-fps </property>
    </quotient>
   </product>
  </sum>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/pitch-gearing">
  <max> <value> 0.02 </value> <min> <value> 1 </value> <quotient> <property> simple/back-moment </property> <property> simple/tail-power </property> </quotient> </min> </max>
 </function>

 <!--
  The same for forward stick. Its power at full travel is the back stick's times the travel down over the
  travel up. Slow, that holds a nose-down angle of attack (the forward angle); fast, the share is what
  holds the negative g limit.
 -->
 <function name="simple/forward-power">
  <quotient> <product> <property> simple/tail-power </property> <property> simple/elevator-down-deg </property> </product> <property> simple/elevator-up-deg </property> </quotient>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/forward-angle-rad">
  <min>
   <product> <property> simple/g-limit-push </property> <property> simple/angle-per-g-rad </property> </product>
   <asin>
    <max> <value> -1 </value> <min> <value> 1 </value>
     <difference>
      <quotient> <property> simple/forward-power </property> <product> <value> -1 </value> <property> simple/nose-stiffness </property> </product> </quotient>
      <sin> <property> simple/trim-angle-rad </property> </sin>
     </difference>
    </min> </max>
   </asin>
  </min>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/forward-g">
  <quotient> <property> simple/forward-angle-rad </property> <property> simple/angle-per-g-rad </property> </quotient>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/forward-moment">
  <sum>
   <product>
    <value> -1 </value> <property> simple/nose-stiffness </property>
    <sum> <sin> <property> simple/forward-angle-rad </property> </sin> <sin> <property> simple/trim-angle-rad </property> </sin> </sum>
   </product>
   <product>
    <value> -1 </value> <property> simple/pitch-damping </property> <property> simple/c2v </property>
    <quotient>
     <product> <value> 32.174 </value> <max> <value> 0 </value> <difference> <property> simple/forward-g </property> <quotient> <value> 1 </value> <max> <property> simple/forward-g </property> <value> 1 </value> </max> </quotient> </difference> </max> </product>
     <property> simple/v-fps </property>
    </quotient>
   </product>
  </sum>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/push-gearing">
  <max> <value> 0.02 </value> <min> <value> 1 </value> <quotient> <property> simple/forward-moment </property> <property> simple/forward-power </property> </quotient> </min> </max>
 </function>

 <!--
  The speed curve in roll: 1 up to the roll rate's speed, then falling as one over the airspeed (Wings: the
  square root of the dynamic pressure at that speed over the dynamic pressure now).
 -->
 <function name="simple/roll-gearing">
  <min>
   <value> 1 </value>
   <pow>
    <quotient>
     <product> <property> simple/q-per-kt2 </property> <property> simple/roll-rate-at-kt </property> <property> simple/roll-rate-at-kt </property> </product>
     <max> <property> aero/qbar-psf </property> <value> 1 </value> </max>
    </quotient>
    <value> 0.5 </value>
   </pow>
  </min>
 </function>

 <!--
  The nosewheel's share of the tilt at this ground speed: 1 standing still, a half at 28 knots, falling on
  a smooth curve (FSG's wheelShare: 1 over 1 plus the speed over 28 knots, cubed).
 -->
 <function name="simple/wheel-share">
  <quotient>
   <value> 1 </value>
   <sum>
    <value> 1 </value>
    <pow>
     <quotient> <property> velocities/vg-fps </property> <product> <value> 28 </value> <value> 1.6878 </value> </product> </quotient>
     <value> 3 </value>
    </pow>
   </sum>
  </quotient>
 </function>

 <channel name="Ground">
  <!--
   On the ground or in the air: 1 with any wheel on the ground, 0 with none, crossing from one to the other
   in a third of a second at a steady rate. The swap's time is this one number.
  -->
  <kinematic name="simple/on-the-ground">
   <input> gear/wow </input>
   <traverse>
    <setting>
     <position> 0 </position>
     <time> 0 </time>
    </setting>
    <setting>
     <position> 1 </position>
     <time> 0.3333 </time>
    </setting>
   </traverse>
  </kinematic>
  <fcs_function name="simple/in-the-air">
   <function>
    <difference> <value> 1 </value> <property> simple/on-the-ground </property> </difference>
   </function>
  </fcs_function>
 </channel>

 <channel name="Pitch">
  <!-- The stick and the trim, summed before the curve: -1 full back to 1 full forward (JSBSim's sense). -->
  <summer name="simple/pitch-asked">
   <input> fcs/elevator-cmd-norm </input>
   <input> fcs/pitch-trim-cmd-norm </input>
   <clipto>
    <min> -1 </min>
    <max> 1 </max>
   </clipto>
  </summer>
  <!-- The stabilator, degrees, trailing edge down positive: back stick through its curve to the travel up, forward stick through its own to the travel down. -->
  <fcs_function name="simple/stabilator-deg">
   <function>
    <ifthen>
     <lt> <property> simple/pitch-asked </property> <value> 0 </value> </lt>
     <product> <property> simple/pitch-asked </property> <property> simple/elevator-up-deg </property> <property> simple/pitch-gearing </property> </product>
     <product> <property> simple/pitch-asked </property> <property> simple/elevator-down-deg </property> <property> simple/push-gearing </property> </product>
    </ifthen>
   </function>
   <output> fcs/elevator-pos-deg </output>
  </fcs_function>
 </channel>

 <channel name="Roll">
  <summer name="simple/roll-asked">
   <input> fcs/aileron-cmd-norm </input>
   <input> fcs/roll-trim-cmd-norm </input>
   <clipto>
    <min> -1 </min>
    <max> 1 </max>
   </clipto>
  </summer>
  <!-- The left aileron, degrees, trailing edge down for a roll to the right; the right one the other way. In the air only: on the ground the ailerons are neutral. -->
  <fcs_function name="simple/left-aileron-deg">
   <function>
    <product> <property> simple/roll-asked </property> <property> simple/aileron-deg </property> <property> simple/roll-gearing </property> <property> simple/in-the-air </property> </product>
   </function>
   <output> fcs/left-aileron-pos-deg </output>
  </fcs_function>
  <pure_gain name="simple/right-aileron-deg">
   <input> simple/left-aileron-deg </input>
   <gain> -1 </gain>
   <output> fcs/right-aileron-pos-deg </output>
  </pure_gain>
 </channel>

 <channel name="Yaw">
  <!--
   What the rudder is asked, -1 to 1, positive nose left (JSBSim's sense): in the air what the copilot's feet
   ask, on the ground the tilt (right tilt, nose right), and across the swap a share of each.
  -->
  <fcs_function name="simple/rudder-asked">
   <function>
    <difference>
     <product> <property> simple/in-the-air </property> <property> fcs/rudder-cmd-norm </property> </product>
     <product> <property> simple/on-the-ground </property> <property> simple/roll-asked </property> </product>
    </difference>
   </function>
   <clipto>
    <min> -1 </min>
    <max> 1 </max>
   </clipto>
  </fcs_function>
  <!-- The rudder, degrees, trailing edge left positive: where it is asked, to its travel. -->
  <fcs_function name="simple/rudder-pos-deg">
   <function>
    <product> <property> simple/rudder-asked </property> <property> simple/rudder-deg </property> </product>
   </function>
   <output> fcs/rudder-pos-deg </output>
  </fcs_function>
 </channel>

 <channel name="Steering">
  <!-- The nosewheel, -1 to 1 of its travel, positive to the right: on the ground the tilt, by the nosewheel's share at this speed; in the air straight. -->
  <fcs_function name="simple/steer-asked">
   <function>
    <product> <property> simple/roll-asked </property> <property> simple/on-the-ground </property> <property> simple/wheel-share </property> </product>
   </function>
   <output> fcs/steer-cmd-norm </output>
  </fcs_function>
 </channel>

</system>
